WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.079
Maybe you’re 35-years-old and you’ve never
had a job.

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:41.610
And one of the things that’s stopping you
is that you’re so damned nervous that you

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:46.050
can’t pick up a phone and use it, because
if anything unexpected happens while you’re

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.780
talking, you get scared so badly you have
to hang up.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.839
And so, that would be characteristic of somebody
with a severe anxiety disorder.

00:00:51.839 --> 00:00:53.210
So, what do you do with that person?

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.920
So, you say: Oh, are they afraid of the phone?

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.390
Well, no.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.390
What are they afraid of?

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.649
Well, they’re afraid of anomalies in human
interaction, right?

00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:05.300
And they’re afraid of something unexpected
happening while they’re trying to impose

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.300
a structure.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.720
And the reason they’re afraid of that is
‘cause they never learned that they were

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.640
capable of dealing with the emergence of something
new on an ongoing basis, which meant that…

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:21.130
Well, which meant in all likelihood that,
when they were children, they were so sheltered

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:26.770
from any contact with those aspects of being
that transcend knowledge that they never learned

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.570
that there was something inside of them that
would reveal itself if they were allowed the

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:37.670
opportunity to encounter the unknown; to encounter
fear, then to master it, then to extract out

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:38.670
something of value.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.360
So, they can’t get a job because they can’t
use the phone.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.660
And it isn’t because they can’t use the
phone, right?

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.510
It’s a deeper story than that.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:50.759
It’s because they’re terrified of this
and they have no idea that they have some

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:54.240
resource they could draw on to combat it.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.479
And you could say: Well, we can teach them
to use the phone, right?

00:01:57.479 --> 00:02:03.030
Say: Well, you know, here’s a repertoire
of stock lines that you might use, you know,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.680
like small talk at a party.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.319
And that would increase what they know and
enable them to deal with the unknown.

00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:12.060
Or you could say: Look, you know, relax a
little bit.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.390
When the person says something on the phone
that you don’t understand, pause a little

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:17.390
bit.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.390
Think about what they’re saying.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:19.600
Pay attention to them.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.840
Allow yourself the luxury of formulating a
response.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.450
You’ll do fine.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:27.340
And even if you do it badly the first half
a dozen times, well, you’ll learn eventually.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:32.750
And the person derives from that the notion
that, not only can they cope on the phone,

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:42.260
but that possibly, there’s more to them
that originally met the eye

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:49.070
We come into the world equipped with an array
of… possibilities and limitations.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:53.920
And those possibilities and limitations are
expressed most particularly in our physical

00:02:53.920 --> 00:03:00.250
form; the fact that we have a specific kind
of embodied form that allows us to do certain

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.720
things, and it also doesn’t allow us to
do certain other things.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:12.840
Lao Tzu has pointed out that it’s the space
inside a pot that makes the pot worthwhile.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:13.840
And…

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:20.340
what he means by that is that… those things
that limit you give you as much form and possibility,

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.340
as those things that enable you.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.490
What that means, more broadly, is the fact
that you’ve come to a given circumstance

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.380
with a set of possibilities and a set of limitations.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:34.920
It’s that, that allows you to impose form
on what you encounter, so that the whole notion

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.590
of form is a tenet in some peculiar way that
we don’t really understand yet on the necessity

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.370
of limitation.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.870
And I think the best way to understand that,
or to begin to understand that, is to give

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.020
some consideration to the notion of a game.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.630
So, for example, if you’re playing chess,
there’s a virtually unlimited number of

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.990
things you can’t do, and only a very narrow
number of things that you can do.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:02.660
Yet, when you’re playing chess, the arbitrary
limitations that are imposed on each piece

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:06.060
don’t seem to be unfair in any sort of cosmic
sense.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.680
They seem to be part of the structure that
enables you to actually play the game.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.319
Without the imposition of those rules, which
are of course relatively arbitrary in the

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:16.810
structure, there wouldn’t be a game at all.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:23.930
Now, it’s clear that, for human beings,
games and fantasy for that matter shade up

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:28.680
into reality, so that the game structures
that we engage in and the fantasy structures

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:34.310
that we use to under­gird our stories and
our pretend play, say when we’re children,

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:36.770
shade imperceptibly into real life.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:41.750
We­We play games because there’s something
about games that make them deeply analogous

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.000
to what we do in day­to­day situations.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.490
And that means, the observation that the rules
of a game actually make the game possible

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:55.139
is an observation that’s broadly applicable
to consideration of your own limitation

00:04:55.139 --> 00:05:00.210
Some of those limitations and possibilities
take the form of emotions and motivations,

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:06.990
and we know, I think, incontrovertibly regardless
of the claims of social scientists who are

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:11.919
more relativist in their orientation, that
human beings come into the world with a standard

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:16.479
set of biological predispositions – emotions
and motivations.

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:21.180
And furthermore, I think we know that it’s
the fact of those shared emotions and motivations

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:28.259
that allow us to communicate at all.

00:05:28.259 --> 00:05:34.380
And then, imagine further that a consequence
of that… lengthy process of interpersonal

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:40.270
negotiation is the emergence of a tremendously
complicated game, and not one that’s arbitrary,

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:44.099
because the game has to have certain rules
in order for it to be played at all.

00:05:44.099 --> 00:05:50.229
So, for example, we know that even with rats,
If rats-- They like to engage in rough-and-tumble

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:55.819
play, and if you put two rats together – juvenile
rats – one rat almost always dominates the

00:05:55.819 --> 00:05:56.819
other.

00:05:56.819 --> 00:06:02.159
It only takes about a 10% gain in weight on
the part of one rat for it to be pretty much

00:06:02.159 --> 00:06:03.620
stability dominant.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:09.150
And it’s always the subordinate rat that
introduces play, or-or-or uh, asks for play.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:15.800
But it turns out that even among rats, if
the dominant rat pins, or… or-or obtains

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:20.790
victory over the subordinate rat more than
70% of the time, the subordinate rat will

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:22.150
no longer play

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.770
So then, you could imagine likewise that if
you’re going to play a game with some other

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:31.389
person, whether it’s a game of fantasy or
the actual chance to engage in some cooperative

00:06:31.389 --> 00:06:37.520
real-world activity, unless that person allows
you a certain amount of space for the manifestation

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.080
of your own emotional and motivational needs,
you’re not going to play the game with them,

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:42.080
right?

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:43.080
You’re going to look for another game.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:47.840
And that means that there’s a certain set
of difficult-to-describe constraints for all

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:55.930
of you on games that you’re willing to play
before you’ll look for another game.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:07:02.659
Well, and then, you could start to conceive
of revolutionary tendencies in that sense,

00:07:02.659 --> 00:07:03.659
right?

00:07:03.659 --> 00:07:08.229
Imagine a human society that’s got so unstable
that the vast majority of the citizens within

00:07:08.229 --> 00:07:10.409
that society are subjugated to starvation.

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:14.509
So, the society never­­ no longer provides
their basic needs.

00:07:14.509 --> 00:07:18.240
And constant tyranny, you could imagine as
well, that there’s going to be an innate

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.440
tendency among the members of that society
to start hypothesizing about what alternatives

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.139
might be possible, right; to start dreaming
about alternative societies, and then also,

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:31.560
to take action if the situation becomes too
extreme.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:35.550
And I think this is part of the reason why
you see stable mythological motifs across

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:40.889
different cultures It’s not so much like
Jung said that we have archetypes of what

00:07:40.889 --> 00:07:44.759
might constitute social order, deeply embedded
in our unconscious.

00:07:44.759 --> 00:07:51.149
I think this­this situation is more externalized
than that in that, what’s biological is

00:07:51.149 --> 00:07:55.059
what we bring to the situation – our hopes
and our desires, and the fact that we have

00:07:55.059 --> 00:07:56.099
hopes and desires.

00:07:56.099 --> 00:08:01.889
Now, I think what happens with stories is
something like this; is that, as human societies

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:05.889
increase in complexity and numbers – so,
they become bigger and bigger – and more

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:11.120
and more people engage in the negotiating
process, exchanging emotional and motivation

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:15.590
information, the pattern that this society
takes, if it’s going to be stable across

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:19.689
long periods of time, starts to become encoded
in the stories.

00:08:19.689 --> 00:08:22.960
So imagine, you got your emotions and your
motivations.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:24.550
You make your case known.

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:29.339
Ten thousand other people do that over a thousand
years, and structure starts to emerge that

00:08:29.339 --> 00:08:35.430
satisfies, more or less, all of these emotions
and motivational states, and is also recognizable

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:41.529
as a pattern

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:46.310
So then, you could say, for example, considering
a story like Moses and the imposition of the

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:52.149
Ten Commandments on the ancient Hebrews, prior
to his imposition of those Ten Commandments

00:08:52.149 --> 00:08:56.680
– of course, there were actually many more
than ten, Moses - according to the mythological

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:03.210
story – Moses spent years, literally years,
adjudicating conflict between the people that

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:04.210
he was leading.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:08.460
18 hours a day, they’d come to him with
their various problems, saying: We have a

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:09.460
dispute.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:10.460
How should it be settled?

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:11.460
And he’d settle it.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:16.540
Well, imagine doing that for ten years, right;
becoming an expert at evaluating what constitutes

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:20.740
an appropriate solution to an emotional problem
between two people.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:25.570
Imagine, as well, that as a consequence of
doing that for such a long period of time,

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:31.690
you start to became-- become to abstract out,
lawful regularities in the manner in which

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:35.380
people have to interact in order for peace
to be maintained.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:39.890
Imagine further that those could be codified
in stories but, even more, codified as law

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:45.361
eventually when consciousness became capable
of grasping, explicitly, the nature of the

00:09:45.361 --> 00:09:46.361
interactions

00:09:46.361 --> 00:09:50.870
So, my point is a point very much like Nietzsche’s
– because Nietzsche said at the end of the

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:56.420
19th Century that it’s a mistake to presume
that most of our philosophies are rational

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:57.529
in nature.

00:09:57.529 --> 00:10:02.670
And I think that’s a mistake that characterizes
Western philosophical thinking, at least since

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:03.670
the Enlightenment.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:09.620
It’s a mistake to assume that there was
a chaotic social state upon which a rational

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:12.709
order was imposed as a consequence of rational
action.

00:10:12.709 --> 00:10:18.220
It’s much more reasonable to presuppose
that the order emerged naturally, over lengthy

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:23.449
periods of time, and then was interpreted
and codified and given structure in a secondary

00:10:23.449 --> 00:10:24.449
manner.

00:10:24.449 --> 00:10:28.640
And so, what I’m trying to outline for you
in large part is the processes by which that

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:29.800
order comes to be.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.470
So, you say, first, it’s behavioural, emotional,
motivational.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:37.980
People, and even animals, communicate in a
way that makes their motivational and emotional

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.120
needs known to one another.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:43.721
So, even among wolves and chimpanzees and
any kind of lower-order social animal, you

00:10:43.721 --> 00:10:49.180
have the emergence of dominance hierarchies,
fundamentally, which are stable solutions

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:53.269
to the entire set of emotional and motivational
problems that besets the group.

00:10:53.269 --> 00:10:58.440
And no one would ever say that the emergence
of a chimpanzee dominance hierarchy is a consequence

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.750
of rational deliberation.

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:04.769
So then, imagine that chimpanzees get the
power to watch what they’re doing and to

00:11:04.769 --> 00:11:06.310
start to represent it.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:11.520
And then, imagine furthermore that that representation
takes the form of stories and, then, laws.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.670
And then, you have some idea about h-how human
social order comes to be.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:20.399
And this is an exciting possibility for me
because it offers-- it offers the potential

00:11:20.399 --> 00:11:27.330
solution to one major question, which is:
How is it that people should act?

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:31.699
And because we’re essentially rationalist
in our presuppositions, we believe that there

00:11:31.699 --> 00:11:33.879
are rationalist solutions to that.

00:11:33.879 --> 00:11:39.850
But the solutions may be something that are
much more akin to biological solutions.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:47.370
So, first, I want you to consider the following
hypothesis.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:51.569
OK, so, we’re going to make the presupposition
– a couple of presuppositions that I don’t

00:11:51.569 --> 00:11:55.139
think are unreasonable – first, is that
we are essentially animals, that we’ve evolved

00:11:55.139 --> 00:12:01.709
in a Darwinian fashion; and second, that the
consequence of that evolution - much of which

00:12:01.709 --> 00:12:06.269
was precognitive, right – uh, that we-we
were around as creatures before we were capable

00:12:06.269 --> 00:12:11.060
of thinking in words, to say, much of that
evolutionary history has conditioned the manner

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:12.060
in which we think.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:15.660
So, we think more like biological entities
than we think like computers.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:19.940
Or, we think more like biological entities
than we think like rational machines.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:24.980
But of course, we already know that rational
machines cannot think very well, except in

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:29.180
very bounded environments, ‘cause they don’t
have access to an embodied structure or to

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:31.230
emotions and motivations.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:32.800
So, let’s…

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:39.399
So then, you might say: Well, why is it that,
like, in the Mesopotamian and Egyptian creation

00:12:39.399 --> 00:12:46.149
myths, our most fundamental representations
of the world tend to take story form and that,

00:12:46.149 --> 00:12:51.279
not only do they take story form, but they
tend to utilize certain kinds of categories.

00:12:51.279 --> 00:12:53.870
Now, I’ve showed you this diagram before.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:58.579
Uhmm, this represents what I think are the
three cardinal categories of experience.

00:12:58.579 --> 00:13:02.540
So, there’s the great mother who’s holding
the world in her hand here.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.769
And inside her subordinate – in a sense
– is the great father.

00:13:06.769 --> 00:13:09.190
This is a Christian representation obviously.

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:15.949
And then, the tragic son of course, in the
crowd here representing society, is adoring

00:13:15.949 --> 00:13:22.319
the figure of the tragic hero here because
they regard his mode of being as necessary

00:13:22.319 --> 00:13:26.790
to their own salvation so to speak – their
own proper mode of living.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.690
OK, so then, you think: Well, why these characters?

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:37.100
And I’d offer you this possibility is, first
of all, every human being that’s ever lived

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:41.279
has lived in an environment characterized
by the presence of these three entities, right?

00:13:41.279 --> 00:13:42.389
We have mothers.

00:13:42.389 --> 00:13:43.389
We have fathers.

00:13:43.389 --> 00:13:45.550
And we exist as individuals.

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:50.070
And then, you think: Well, when you’re a
child and you begin to comprehend the world,

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:53.819
the outside world, everything outside of the
family is of course vague and ill-defined,

00:13:53.819 --> 00:13:56.410
right, non-existent in a sense.

00:13:56.410 --> 00:14:03.070
And all there is for you to observe is the
mother, who for you really is the whole world,

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:08.510
and the father, a-a secondary source of comfort
and trouble perhaps, and the fact of your

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:09.510
own individuality.

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:13.980
And so, you say: Well, that’s true from
the perspective of individual development.

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:19.139
But then, if you go way, way back in history
– maybe 500,000 years when our cognitive

00:14:19.139 --> 00:14:22.499
capacities were first starting to develop
– and we were trying to figure out what

00:14:22.499 --> 00:14:27.860
the world was really like, what categories
would we have at our disposal to start to

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:31.209
modify and change in order to represent the
outside world.

00:14:31.209 --> 00:14:36.040
And then, you might think: You can only talk
about what you don’t know in terms that

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:37.610
you know.

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:40.839
And since, for the child, the mother is the
world.

00:14:40.839 --> 00:14:48.060
It isn’t absurd to presume that for the
human being, the world is the mother, first,

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:56.019
as a projection, right, as an a priori, cognitive
schema.

00:14:56.019 --> 00:15:02.050
The hypothesis being: The natural world which
of course manifest itself in truth in the

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:06.959
mother – partakes in many ways of the same
properties as the mother.

00:15:06.959 --> 00:15:12.351
It’s a working hypothesis, just like you
might presume if you date a new woman, that

00:15:12.351 --> 00:15:17.060
she has aspects of your sister – all things
considered – aspects of your mother, given

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:18.800
that they were also female.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:23.420
You take what you know to represent what you
don’t know.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:30.100
So, we use our fundamental, social cognitive
categories initially to portray the world.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:34.839
And the world’s nature is portray and personified,
or metaphorical form.

00:15:34.839 --> 00:15:38.129
And so then, the question is: What are the
primary categories?

00:15:38.129 --> 00:15:40.529
Well, we have three of them here.

00:15:40.529 --> 00:15:44.329
But they’re not the only three because this
is all good, this category system – the

00:15:44.329 --> 00:15:47.310
benevolent mother, the benevolent father,
and the hero.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:51.499
Well, we know the world is not only benevolent,
it’s also malevolent.

00:15:51.499 --> 00:15:56.749
Or at least, we can say that because we’re
equipped with certain emotional possibilities

00:15:56.749 --> 00:16:03.709
and certain motivational possibilities the
probability that we will encounter despair

00:16:03.709 --> 00:16:09.550
and frustration and disappointment, and anxiety
is just as real as the possibility that we

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:11.980
will encounter and hope, and satisfaction.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:14.680
So, for us, the world is bivalent.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:16.750
It takes with one and it gives with the other.

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:21.790
And that’s true for the natural world, which
produces us and destroys; as it is for the

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:26.980
social world, which fosters our development
and crushes our individuality; and as well

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:31.790
for the individual, who in many regards is
as admirable a creature as you can hope ever

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:37.379
to propose and, at the same time, someone
who’s capable of unbelievable depths of

00:16:37.379 --> 00:16:39.189
depravity.

00:16:39.189 --> 00:16:46.180
So… a world that’s not only divided into
three fundamental categories, but each category

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:52.089
divided into a structure that’s essentially
ambivalent in its fundamental element.

00:16:52.089 --> 00:16:57.430
And of course, that poses the central existential
problem for human existence, doesn’t it?

00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:02.439
I mean, we’re faced with the vagaries of
the natural world and what we don’t understand;

00:17:02.439 --> 00:17:06.780
he vagaries of the social world and its often
arbitrary and unreasonable demands on us;

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:11.980
and the fact of our capacity for transcendence,
tied to our own vulnerability.

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:16.380
And you could say perfectly reasonably that,
regardless of where you’re situated in time

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.829
and space, those are basically your problems.

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:23.240
And your goal through life – your path through
life – is going to be characterized by the

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:28.680
solutions you either come up with or don’t
come up with to that set of problems.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:38.530
OK, so, there’s one more categorical element
that complicates this picture.

00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:42.280
And I think, in many ways, it’s the most
difficult thing there is to grasp.

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:44.440
So, we’ll take a shot at it first.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:48.410
And I’ve showed you this representation
before.

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:54.520
This is the dragon of chaos, and you can think
of the dragon of chaos as a symbol of totality.

00:17:54.520 --> 00:18:02.530
And furthermore, you can think of it in relationship
to this structure…

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:09.290
as the source of this structure… or even
as the source of all structure.

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:14.440
So, you see in the Sumerian creation myth,
for example, the character of Tiamat, right?

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:19.770
And I told you that the world Tiamat is associated
with the later Hebrew word “Taom”, which

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:20.770
means chaos.

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:25.190
And Taom is the chaos that Yahweh makes order
out of; makes the world out of.

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:29.900
So, the idea lurking behind the Sumerian creation
myth - and then, later, lurking behind the

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:35.620
entire edifice of Judaism-Christianity and
Islam for that matter - is that something

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:41.350
that can be best represented by this figure
is best conceptualized as the ground of everything

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:42.350
that exists.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:44.540
Now, what in the world can that mean?

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.950
Well, it means something like this.

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:50.150
Let’s look at the concrete metaphorical
representation.

00:18:50.150 --> 00:18:53.700
And first of all, you have a kind of totality
here, right?

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:57.810
You have a thing that can live by devouring
itself.

00:18:57.810 --> 00:19:00.531
So, it has no need of anything outside of
it.

00:19:00.531 --> 00:19:02.470
In fact, there is nothing outside of it.

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:05.460
It’s a figure of absolute totality.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:11.330
And it’s characterized by a strange intermixture
of metaphorical representations of matter

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:15.720
– because a snake is something that crawls
on the ground – and spirit – because a

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:20.390
winged serpent is something that can fly and,
therefore, partakes of the metaphorical realm

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:23.960
of heaven - heaven and earth, right?

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:26.160
Totality--Yin and Yang, from the Daoist perspective.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:28.290
That’s the entire world.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:32.680
And it’s also something that’s characterized
by the capacity for transformation because

00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:35.380
a snake can shed its skin and be reborn.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:41.680
So, it’s something that’s constantly renewing
itself, despite its absolutely archaic age.

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:46.570
And it’s also something that presents a
terrible danger and tremendous opportunity,

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:50.930
because a dragon is something that will burn
you if you get anywhere near it, but also

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:55.280
hordes a treasure that’s more valuable than
anything else.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:20:00.270
Eliade has pointed out that in traditional,
classic creation myths - the Sumerian myth

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:08.710
being one example, the hero when he encounters
the first the great dragon of chaos he first

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:12.390
either runs away or is paralyzed by fright.

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:17.490
The world in itself is a complex array of
patterns.

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:22.330
And those patterns manifest themselves in
space, and they manifest themselves in time.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:26.840
And I think the best way to get a grip on
what those patterns might be like is to think

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:28.650
of them in terms of music.

00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:30.860
And I think that’s what music represents.

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:38.300
Music is this complex three-dimensional structure,
full of interwoven patterns of different dimensions

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:41.220
and length that expends itself over time.

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:45.560
And if you listen to a piece of music, you
can concentrate on one instrument or another,

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:49.700
or you can concentrate on a phrase, or you
can concentrate on the entire melody or the

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:50.700
voice.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:53.930
You can parse out different elements from
the complex background.

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:57.670
And that’s especially the case with very
sophisticated orchestral music, right, which

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:04.270
is susceptible to multiple reinterpretations,
and multiple encounters, because of its complexity.

00:21:04.270 --> 00:21:11.090
And this is to say only that what you look
at is far more complicated than what you see.

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:15.810
Or to say, alternatively, that there’s more
information in anything you perceive than

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.960
you can ever get complete access to.

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:25.150
And that’s partly because, It’s partly
because your perceptual systems delude you.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:29.150
So, we think you look with your eyes or with
your other senses.

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:32.520
But that’s only true when you’re looking
at what you already know what to look at,

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:33.520
right?

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:38.320
When you’ve already built perceptual machinery
that enables you to detect a particular object.

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:42.780
But when you’re looking at what you don’t
know what to look at, the way you look is

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:44.170
by getting nervous, right?

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:46.610
It’s not-- It’s not precisely a perceptual
function.

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:50.130
It’s something much more deeper and primordial
than that.

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:53.280
And it’s more like: Oh no.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:56.910
Something that I cannot categorize – either
perceptually or cognitively – something

00:21:56.910 --> 00:22:00.370
that I do not know how to respond to has just
occurred.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:03.430
And the first categorization is this: That’s
it.

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:05.080
There’s-There’s nothing under that.

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:09.740
It’s merely fear plus heightened attention.

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:13.870
And that prepares the ground for constructing
a more detailed representation.

00:22:13.870 --> 00:22:25.080
But that first encounter, that’s the encounter
with the dragon of chaos.

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:30.890
So then, you take this figure – the source
of all things, the… the Dao in some ways

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:35.650
– and you say: Well, how does it manifest
itself?

00:22:35.650 --> 00:22:46.600
And the answer to that is something like this:
And, we see this both in the-- in-in the Mesopotamian

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:50.200
and the Egyptian myths that I described to
you.

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:54.760
The first division of the great dragon of
chaos, or the primordial egg is always into

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:59.670
two subordinate elements: The great father,
and the great mother.

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:00.990
Why is that?

00:23:00.990 --> 00:23:06.960
Well, it’s illustrative of a fundamental--
of the fundamental binary nature of existence,

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:14.320
I guess; partly, you could say: If you’re
a cognizant being – a defined delimited

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:18.610
being – what you perceive always has a binary
structure.

00:23:18.610 --> 00:23:22.370
There’s the aspect of you that structured
enough to allow the perceiving.

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:23.690
That’s what you know.

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:28.120
That’s-That’s the manner in which you’re
structured so that you can even… so that-that

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:33.970
you can even formulate a perception – something
a child builds up over time from the primordial

00:23:33.970 --> 00:23:39.390
aspect, say, of his visual system or his auditory
system; learns to parse up the world by generating

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:44.000
machinery that allows the complex patterns
that make up the world to be turned into objects.

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:48.720
So, there’s the thing - the structure that
allows the perceiving and then, there’s

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:51.770
the thing that’s being perceived

00:23:51.770 --> 00:23:52.900
or the thing behind that.

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:55.060
And this is a very complicated distinction.

00:23:55.060 --> 00:24:00.290
You can say: Well, what’s the difference
between the dragon of chaos - say, the representation

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:06.070
of the cosmos as such - and the great mother?

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:09.920
And I would say, it’s something like this…

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:16.090
The unknown that appears in relationship to
a perceiver is different than the unknown

00:24:16.090 --> 00:24:17.090
as such.

00:24:17.090 --> 00:24:21.730
So, I would say this, for example: There are
going to be things that surprise you that

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:24.260
wouldn’t surprise me, and vice versa.

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:28.950
And the things that would surprise you have
to be construed in relationship to what you--

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:33.450
to what you already know, ‘cause you’re
only going to be surprised by things you don’t

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:34.450
know.

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:37.510
And likewise for me, I’m only going to be
surprised by things I don’t know.

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:39.580
But what we know is going to vary somewhat.

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:44.700
So, the unknown for you is going to be different
than the unknown for me.

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:51.250
And the great mother is a representation of
the unknown for you, for the unknown for me.

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:55.390
Different for everyone in some sense, because
we’re all going to be…

00:24:55.390 --> 00:25:00.980
We’re all …all-all going to be stymied
and stalked by different aspects of being;

00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:05.850
but the same, as well, in that when you encounter
things you don’t understand, and you encounter

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:11.260
things you don’t understand, in many, many
ways, you’re going to react to those different

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:12.750
things the same way.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:15.470
And I can give you a narrative illustration
of this.

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:19.370
King Arthur’s knights-- They sit around
the round table.

00:25:19.370 --> 00:25:22.590
They’re all equals, that’s why they sit
around the round table, right?

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:23.590
They have a king.

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:26.070
The king determines their destinies, like
Marduk does.

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:27.160
But they’re still all equal.

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:29.250
They determine they’re going to go look
for the Holy Grail.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:30.850
It’s the symbol of redemption.

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:37.030
So, they’re off to find the highest value,
like, Pinocchio wishing on a star - the highest

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:38.030
value.

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:41.410
And they all enter the forest to begin their
quest, but they each enter it at the place

00:25:41.410 --> 00:25:43.390
that appears darkest to each of them, right?

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:46.870
So, that means they all go on different--
They all go in different directions; even

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:51.660
though they’re on the same quest that theoretically,
they’re inhabiting the same space.

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:58.040
So… so, it’s only to say that every person
has their demons, so to speak, and that those

00:25:58.040 --> 00:26:02.540
demons differ from person to person, even
though there are things you can say about

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:04.950
the demons that are common across people.

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:08.620
So we know, for example, from clinical work
– from endless clinical work – that if

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:12.340
you want to help someone, you identify: OK,
what do you want to do?

00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:13.810
‘cause they need to know that, right?

00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:14.810
And that’s this.

00:26:14.810 --> 00:26:15.810
What do you want to do?

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:16.810
Where do you want to go?

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:18.990
What kind of structure do you want to impose
on your world?

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:20.754
And then, you identify: OK, well, what things
are stopping you?

00:26:20.754 --> 00:26:22.390
What things are stopping you?

00:26:22.390 --> 00:26:27.900
So, then, you look back at the Sumerian creation
myth and you think: Well, there’s Absu,

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:28.900
right?

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:30.890
God of the Known, Tiamet’s Consort, right?

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:36.480
Culture, but there’s also Marduk, and Marduk
is the power – the spirit, the entity – a

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:43.140
representation of the Sumerian saviour who
goes out to confront this and to make the

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:44.140
world.

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:46.900
Well, that’s what you teach people in behavioural
therapy.

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:49.210
You teach them not so much that.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:51.220
You don’t teach them habituation.

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:54.810
You don’t teach them to get used to things
that they’re afraid of.

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:57.790
You teach them that there’s something within
them that can respond to the things that they’re

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:53.320
afraid of - that’s of as great a magnitude
as the fears themselves.

